/**
This program was created as an assignment for Fundamental Data Structures, grade pending. 

A4 Browser History Manager

Assignment:
	Implement a browser navigation system using your previously developed doubly linked list, iterator, and deque classes. 
The program must manage visited pages, the current page, forward-history invalidation, range deletion, and a FIFO queue
of pending visits

Requirements: 
  •	Reuse Data structures as application components.
  •	Use bidirectional iterators for navigation, traversal, insertion, and removal
  •	Preserve valid current-page state during mutations.
  •	Use a deque to process pending navigation cases requests in FIFO order.
  •	Test normal operations and edge cases and explain implementation decisions. 

Implementation:
	I created a class, FoogleHistory with the private members front, rear, and page as pointer variables. The pointer variables 
front and rear act as place holders for the entire list of history with rear holding the place of the oldest page visited and 
front holding the place of the newest page in the list. The variable page points to the URL that is currently being viewed. 
For pending requests, I created a second deque struct, FutureList that is initialized as a private member of FoogleHistory, 
pending. This includes FutureList in FoogleHistory using composition and allows FoogleHistory to manage both the future requests
and the current history. 
	In addition to the required functions, I also added bool historyEmpty() that returns true if front is equal to nullptr. During 
the build and debugging process I used assert((front == nullptr) == (rear == nullptr)) inside historyEmpty() to identify and fix 
cases that created a situation where front == nullptr but rear still pointed to a node. 
	I also added a function void showAll() const; that will print all nodes from rear to front regardless of the URL that page is 
currently pointing to. During debugging this was used to test cases where history was supposed to be deleted, specifically after 
visit when there is a forward history. After visit() is called, all forward history is supposed to be deleted. Using showAll() 
I was able to verify that page = front and front  next == nullptr.

makefile:
	A debug log was created with this program using #include <cassert>. 
    •	make RELEASE=1 
      o	Compile and execute foogle.exe without debug logs
    •	make run: 
      o	compiles and executes foogle.exe
    •	make clean: 
      •	deletes previous object files and foogle.exe  

Required Tests:
T1	Visit three pages --	Chronological order correct
T2	Back twice --	Correct current page
T3	Forward once --	Correct current page
T4	Back from first --	No state change
T5	Forward from last --	No state change
T6	Visit after back --	Forward entries removed
T7	Delete range excluding current --	Current preserved
T8	Delete range containing current -- Replacement follows specified rule
T9	Delete all history --	Empty state safe
T10	Queue/process three URLs --	FIFO order
T11	Process empty queue -- Safe false result
T12	Operate after deleting all history --	Program remains functional
**/
